Let an adapter stand in for a library the application carries.

Brighter Shores links @loader_path/libsteam_api.dylib, Valve's library, which needs
a Steam client the device does not have; pocket/translation/libsteam_api replaces it.
classify.py treated every @-library as bundled (run as the application's own code,
no map entry), so a build made for this executable would neither build the adapter
nor map the library to it. With this change an @-library whose leaf has a
hand-written adapter is planned as a full shim and mapped to it; one without stays
bundled, as before. The runtime opens the mapped adapter when the library is not in
the application's folder (tools/stage_client.py leaves Valve's file out).
Upstream candidate: brand-free, no effect on an application without such an adapter.

--- a/tools/classify.py
+++ b/tools/classify.py
@@ -251,7 +251,10 @@
         has_adapter = d in adapters and any(name.endswith(('.c','.m')) for name in os.listdir(os.path.join(TRANSLATION, d)))
         if l in PSEUDO_LIBRARIES:
             kind = PSEUDO_LIBRARIES[l]
-        elif l.startswith("@"):
+        # A library the application carries runs as its own code, unless an adapter
+        # stands in for it (one that needs a service the device lacks, such as a
+        # desktop client to talk to); the staged application then leaves it out.
+        elif l.startswith("@") and not has_adapter:
             kind = "bundled"
         elif d in absent:
             kind = "absent"; mapping[l] = ""   # the runtime does not open it
